1
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:46,335
AT THE BERLIN CONFERENCE OF 1884,

2
00:00:46,502 --> 00:00:49,755
THE COLONIAL POWERS
CLAIMED THE RIGHT

3
00:00:49,921 --> 00:00:51,882
TO DIVIDE UP THE CONGO.

4
00:00:55,553 --> 00:01:00,725
KING LEOPOLD OF BELGIUM
CLAIMED THE VAST CONGO BASIN,

5
00:01:00,892 --> 00:01:04,687
RICH IN IVORY AND MINERALS.

6
00:01:06,938 --> 00:01:10,694
FIVE YEARS LATER,
HE HAD RUN UP ENORMOUS DEBTS

7
00:01:10,861 --> 00:01:15,031
IN HIS AMBITION
TO EXPLOIT THE NEW COLONY.

8
00:01:19,952 --> 00:01:21,496
DESPERATE FOR FUNDS

9
00:01:21,662 --> 00:01:25,082
AND WITH ALMOST NO MONEY
TO PAY HIS ARMY,

10
00:01:27,335 --> 00:01:30,630
HE SENT HIS MOST TRUSTED
OFFICIAL, LEON ROM,

11
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:32,215
TO THE CONGO

12
00:01:32,382 --> 00:01:34,175
TO OBTAIN
THE LEGENDARY DIAMONDS OF OPAR.

13
00:01:40,890 --> 00:01:43,851
THE CONGO, AFRICA

14
00:02:16,759 --> 00:02:18,428
<i>Monsieur Rom...</i>

15
00:02:24,851 --> 00:02:26,268
Opar.

16
00:02:29,229 --> 00:02:31,482
We found it.

17
00:02:39,907 --> 00:02:41,200
Captain Moulle?

18
00:02:45,955 --> 00:02:47,833
Form ranks.

19
00:02:55,548 --> 00:02:57,300
Maxim guns ready, sir!

20
00:03:03,096 --> 00:03:04,766
<i>Stand fast.</i>

21
00:03:10,605 --> 00:03:12,148
<i>Wait.</i>

22
00:03:20,990 --> 00:03:22,116
Fire!

23
00:03:42,805 --> 00:03:44,014
<i>Hold your positions!</i>

24
00:04:57,294 --> 00:04:58,920
Hold your ground!

25
00:06:36,435 --> 00:06:38,896
Chief Mbonga, I presume?

26
00:06:39,856 --> 00:06:41,774
How do you know my name?

27
00:06:43,693 --> 00:06:45,403
I came here to see you.

28
00:07:02,378 --> 00:07:04,672
This is what you came for.

29
00:07:10,761 --> 00:07:12,638
What would you do to get it?

30
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:16,224
Whatever it takes.

31
00:07:17,726 --> 00:07:22,355
There is only one thing I desire above all else.

32
00:07:23,231 --> 00:07:24,940
Bring him to me.

33
00:07:25,399 --> 00:07:27,819
Bring me that man.

34
00:07:28,404 --> 00:07:30,699
And the diamonds are yours.

35
00:07:35,036 --> 00:07:36,913
All I need is a name.

36
00:07:51,676 --> 00:07:58,517
THE LEGEND OF TARZAN

37
00:08:04,190 --> 00:08:06,609
LONDON, ENGLAND

38
00:08:14,282 --> 00:08:17,787
His Serene Highness,
King Leopold of Belgium...

39
00:08:17,954 --> 00:08:21,124
<i>has invited Lord Greystoke...</i>

40
00:08:21,290 --> 00:08:25,211
<i>to tour the Congo Free State...</i>

41
00:08:25,378 --> 00:08:27,796
<i>to visit the schools
and churches he has built...</i>

42
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:31,925
to witness the success
of his anti-slavery initiatives

43
00:08:32,092 --> 00:08:34,345
and to open trade negotiations.

44
00:08:41,394 --> 00:08:45,897
<i>Lord Greystoke,
allow me to explain the situation in Africa.</i>

45
00:08:49,317 --> 00:08:52,155
King Leopold controls the Congo.

46
00:08:52,822 --> 00:08:54,698
<i>He is defaulting on his loans...</i>

47
00:08:54,865 --> 00:08:57,993
<i>and needs a partner to cover his debts.</i>

48
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:00,871
<i>His invitation is
the perfect opportunity...</i>

49
00:09:01,037 --> 00:09:03,832
<i>for Her Majesty's government
to become involved.</i>

50
00:09:04,833 --> 00:09:08,255
Think of the jobs we could create
for the poor natives of the Congo.

51
00:09:11,090 --> 00:09:13,551
And I thought the Civil War was long.

52
00:09:13,718 --> 00:09:16,012
Dr. Williams, you are a guest here.

53
00:09:16,179 --> 00:09:18,639
<i>Prime Minister, if you won't say it...</i>

54
00:09:20,348 --> 00:09:21,600
allow me to do so.

55
00:09:21,767 --> 00:09:24,520
You're Tarzan!

56
00:09:24,687 --> 00:09:26,105
<i>Lord of the Apes, King of the Jungle.</i>

57
00:09:26,272 --> 00:09:27,857
PUNCH
LORD GREYSTOKE GOES OUT FOR LUNCH...

58
00:09:28,024 --> 00:09:29,858
<i>"Me Tarzan, you Jane!"</i>

59
00:09:30,025 --> 00:09:32,027
Tarzan!

60
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:36,615
King Leopold is inviting you
because you're a celebrity.

61
00:09:36,782 --> 00:09:39,493
<i>He wants to show you
the schools and churches he has built.</i>

62
00:09:39,659 --> 00:09:41,996
<i>You express your amazement
to the newspapers...</i>

63
00:09:42,163 --> 00:09:45,458
and he gets the endorsement
of Africa's favorite son.

64
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:50,337
England pays his debts.
We make money.

65
00:09:50,504 --> 00:09:52,256
Couldn't be simpler!

66
00:09:53,299 --> 00:09:56,010
Your Lordships...

67
00:09:56,177 --> 00:09:59,055
shall we begin?

68
00:10:14,779 --> 00:10:15,780
No.

69
00:10:15,947 --> 00:10:17,573
-No?
-For God's sake, why not?

70
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:20,201
<i>I've been to Africa before.</i>

71
00:10:22,077 --> 00:10:23,161
And it's hot.

72
00:10:23,745 --> 00:10:26,248
Excuse me? Did you say "hot"?

73
00:10:29,793 --> 00:10:32,754
Please convey my apologies
to His Serene Highness.

74
00:10:37,759 --> 00:10:40,262
And my name is not Tarzan.

75
00:10:40,429 --> 00:10:42,431
It's John Clayton III.

76
00:10:42,597 --> 00:10:45,602
<i>Son of John and Lady Alice Clayton.</i>

77
00:10:45,769 --> 00:10:49,814
Fifth Earl of Greystoke,
member of the House of Lords.

78
00:10:56,820 --> 00:10:58,488
<i>Mr. Clayton.</i>

79
00:10:58,655 --> 00:11:01,158
<i>Lord Clayton. My apologies.</i>

80
00:11:01,325 --> 00:11:04,203
George Washington Williams.
United States diplomatic envoy.

81
00:11:04,370 --> 00:11:06,663
-President Harrison sent me.
 -To do what?

82
00:11:06,830 --> 00:11:08,331
To accompany the Queen's emissary.

83
00:11:08,498 --> 00:11:11,835
The U.S. was the first to recognize
King Leopold's claim to the Congo.

84
00:11:12,461 --> 00:11:14,795
It wouldn't want to miss out on the spoils.

85
00:11:14,962 --> 00:11:16,130
Look...

86
00:11:17,298 --> 00:11:20,178
I need your invitation to take part.

87
00:11:20,344 --> 00:11:22,721
I'm afraid my intentions aren't

88
00:11:22,888 --> 00:11:25,057
what I told your government or mine.

89
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:30,146
And what exactly do you want, Dr. Williams?

90
00:11:32,064 --> 00:11:36,319
Leopold has spent the last seven years
taking control of the Congo.

91
00:11:36,486 --> 00:11:38,946
He spent his entire fortune
building his railway...

92
00:11:39,113 --> 00:11:41,948
and blocked access to 99% of the country.

93
00:11:42,115 --> 00:11:43,075
Why?

94
00:11:43,241 --> 00:11:45,243
<i>Because he wants to be the first monarch...</i>

95
00:11:45,410 --> 00:11:47,829
<i>who doesn't want people
to see his accomplishments?</i>

96
00:11:47,996 --> 00:11:49,663
<i>I don't believe it.</i>

97
00:11:50,332 --> 00:11:52,001
<i>Tell me something, Lord Clayton.</i>

98
00:11:52,168 --> 00:11:57,131
How does a bankrupt king keep
the entire Congo running?

99
00:11:59,716 --> 00:12:00,842
With slave labor?

100
00:12:02,344 --> 00:12:05,514
I hope I'm wrong, but I need to know.

101
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:08,683
And for the sake of your friends
back in your homeland...

102
00:12:08,850 --> 00:12:09,893
you need to know, too.

103
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:16,108
Accept Leopold's invitation
and take me with you.

104
00:12:16,274 --> 00:12:18,693
You know the remotest parts of Africa.

105
00:12:18,860 --> 00:12:21,363
<i>The languages, the people, the customs.</i>

106
00:12:22,072 --> 00:12:23,408
If we find anything wrong...

107
00:12:23,575 --> 00:12:28,454
I need someone credible
to tell the world.

108
00:12:36,962 --> 00:12:38,714
I'll make all the arrangements.

109
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:40,925
See you in Liverpool.

110
00:12:55,397 --> 00:12:58,276
GREYSTOKE MANOR

111
00:13:14,542 --> 00:13:15,918
<i>My family.</i>

112
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:21,173
<i>I've done everything I can to protect them.</i>

113
00:13:25,052 --> 00:13:28,180
<i>We've been surrounded by predators
since the shipwreck.</i>

114
00:13:30,641 --> 00:13:32,518
<i>The jungle consumes everything.</i>

115
00:13:33,895 --> 00:13:37,939
<i>It devours the old, the sick,
the injured, the weak.</i>

116
00:13:38,106 --> 00:13:40,108
<i>But never the strong.</i>

117
00:13:43,819 --> 00:13:46,323
<i>Today, I buried my Alice.</i>

118
00:13:47,574 --> 00:13:49,494
<i>My dearest Alice.</i>

119
00:13:51,913 --> 00:13:53,288
<i>Now I fear for my son.</i>

120
00:13:56,124 --> 00:13:57,501
<i>May God help us.</i>

121
00:13:58,794 --> 00:14:00,254
<i>May God help him.</i>

122
00:15:15,537 --> 00:15:19,458
<i>The most dangerous beast in Africa
is so powerful...</i>

123
00:15:19,624 --> 00:15:23,044
it can snap a crocodile's back
with a single bite.

124
00:15:25,882 --> 00:15:27,383
Does anyone know what it is?

125
00:15:28,885 --> 00:15:30,886
Is it a hippopotamus?

126
00:15:31,053 --> 00:15:34,681
Yes! That's right. Well done, Mary.

127
00:15:37,351 --> 00:15:38,351
<i>Now...</i>

128
00:15:38,518 --> 00:15:42,231
can anyone tell me
what my favorite bird sounds like?

129
00:15:43,316 --> 00:15:44,358
Anyone?

130
00:16:01,833 --> 00:16:03,377
How do you travel through the jungle?

131
00:16:03,543 --> 00:16:05,169
<i>Why did you leave the jungle?</i>

132
00:16:07,254 --> 00:16:08,255
<i>Look at his hands!</i>

133
00:16:09,423 --> 00:16:11,426
Wow!

134
00:16:12,345 --> 00:16:14,430
I grew up running on all fours.

135
00:16:15,264 --> 00:16:17,600
It changed my bone structure.

136
00:16:21,979 --> 00:16:24,231
<i>Do you swing from things indoors?</i>

137
00:16:24,398 --> 00:16:26,525
Like when you go upstairs?

138
00:16:26,691 --> 00:16:29,611
I never use the stairs.

139
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:31,113
I usually use the curtains.

140
00:16:31,279 --> 00:16:32,406
<i>Amazing!</i>

141
00:16:33,281 --> 00:16:36,076
Is it true your mother was an ape?

142
00:16:37,786 --> 00:16:39,787
Of course not.

143
00:16:39,954 --> 00:16:42,124
My mother was Alice Clayton.

144
00:16:45,836 --> 00:16:47,672
I can't wait to see Wasimbu again.

145
00:16:47,839 --> 00:16:49,256
-How many children will he have?
-Jane.

146
00:16:49,423 --> 00:16:51,466
We know about Keko. How many does he have, four?

147
00:16:51,633 --> 00:16:53,385
<i>-They must all have children by now.
-Jane.</i>

148
00:16:53,552 --> 00:16:55,429
Eight years is a lifetime!

149
00:16:55,595 --> 00:16:56,805
This isn't a holiday.

150
00:16:56,972 --> 00:16:59,182
How many corsets does a diplomat take?

151
00:16:59,349 --> 00:17:01,143
<i>I can't take you with me.</i>

152
00:17:02,310 --> 00:17:04,272
-I'd say none.
-Four.

153
00:17:05,022 --> 00:17:08,400
An Englishwoman ought to faint
twice to justify that silly title.

154
00:17:08,567 --> 00:17:09,568
It's not safe there.

155
00:17:09,735 --> 00:17:11,403
Of course it's not safe, John!

156
00:17:11,570 --> 00:17:15,157
If you sleep in the wrong place,
there's a 50% chance you'll be eaten.

157
00:17:16,408 --> 00:17:18,493
Girls. Albert.

158
00:17:25,667 --> 00:17:28,503
<i>You can't keep thinking
it's dangerous, John.</i>

159
00:17:31,757 --> 00:17:35,009
You haven't said his name in years.

160
00:17:37,846 --> 00:17:39,222
Talk to me.

161
00:17:40,974 --> 00:17:43,184
Mbonga isn't my concern.

162
00:17:43,851 --> 00:17:45,061
You are.

163
00:17:45,686 --> 00:17:47,648
We lost a baby.

164
00:17:47,815 --> 00:17:50,859
The last thing you need is more stress.

165
00:17:53,571 --> 00:17:54,989
Yes.

166
00:17:56,032 --> 00:17:57,784
Thank you for reminding me.

167
00:17:59,117 --> 00:18:01,953
In case you've forgotten,
we both grew up there.

168
00:18:03,288 --> 00:18:05,707
So perhaps I can carry out
your duties for the Queen...

169
00:18:05,874 --> 00:18:07,042
and you can stay here...

170
00:18:07,209 --> 00:18:09,711
crossbreeding coconuts and playing ping-pong.

171
00:18:13,048 --> 00:18:14,800
I want to go home.

172
00:18:14,966 --> 00:18:16,093
This is our home.

173
00:18:19,011 --> 00:18:21,098
Trying to keep me here won't work!

174
00:21:38,879 --> 00:21:41,088
COLONIAL OFFICE
JOHN CLAYTON

175
00:21:45,678 --> 00:21:47,055
<i>John, my son...</i>

176
00:21:47,221 --> 00:21:50,098
<i>you are the heir to Greystoke,
our future.</i>

177
00:21:52,768 --> 00:21:55,520
<i>England is your home,
not this wretched place.</i>

178
00:21:57,146 --> 00:21:58,147
<i>If I do not survive...</i>

179
00:22:00,609 --> 00:22:03,070
<i>I pray someone will rescue you.</i>

180
00:23:00,292 --> 00:23:03,212
Promise me you'll stay
with the Kuba the whole time.

181
00:23:53,888 --> 00:23:55,432
Impossible, ma'am.

182
00:23:55,599 --> 00:23:59,853
The recoil of one of these Walker Colts
can be quite painful.

183
00:24:00,019 --> 00:24:02,104
That's actually why they stopped making them.

184
00:24:02,271 --> 00:24:05,399
Pretty young ladies like you
kept hitting themselves in the face with them.

185
00:24:06,650 --> 00:24:09,570
I bet I can hit that log over there
on my first try.

186
00:24:15,202 --> 00:24:16,411
<i>Jane?</i>

187
00:25:01,249 --> 00:25:03,875
He's known them since they were cubs.

188
00:25:13,801 --> 00:25:16,637
BOMA WELCOMES
JOHN CLAYTON III

189
00:25:34,865 --> 00:25:36,658
Force Publique, move out.

190
00:25:36,824 --> 00:25:39,326
Force Publique, move!

191
00:25:40,911 --> 00:25:42,539
I want the port of entry...

192
00:25:42,706 --> 00:25:45,459
and the landing point for every passenger.

193
00:25:45,624 --> 00:25:47,168
Yes, sir. These are the last ones.

194
00:25:47,334 --> 00:25:49,670
-Sir.
-Thank you.

195
00:25:54,801 --> 00:25:56,428
ARRIVAL
JOHN CLAYTON

196
00:25:56,595 --> 00:25:58,680
ARRIVAL
JANE CLAYTON

197
00:26:05,019 --> 00:26:06,020
Excuse me, sir.

198
00:26:07,189 --> 00:26:08,356
The register.

199
00:26:08,523 --> 00:26:09,983
Yes, thank you.

200
00:26:36,843 --> 00:26:39,721
<i>Take the gramophone
to Mr. Rom's cabin.</i>

201
00:26:42,182 --> 00:26:43,266
<i>Mr. Frum!</i>

202
00:26:44,726 --> 00:26:46,353
Those crates go in the hold.

203
00:26:46,520 --> 00:26:50,481
Champagne? And music?
Your operation has failed.

204
00:26:50,648 --> 00:26:51,690
Have faith, Mr. Frum.

205
00:26:51,857 --> 00:26:53,734
Faith is for missionaries.

206
00:26:53,901 --> 00:26:55,152
He eluded them.

207
00:26:57,029 --> 00:26:59,074
Make sure the rest go below.

208
00:27:00,492 --> 00:27:03,578
They spurned our invitation, Mr. Frum.

209
00:27:03,745 --> 00:27:05,496
They jumped ship near the coast...

210
00:27:05,663 --> 00:27:07,832
and are making their own way home.

211
00:27:08,874 --> 00:27:10,126
BOMA
KUBA TERRITORY

212
00:27:10,292 --> 00:27:12,629
From Boma to here
will take me three days.

213
00:27:12,796 --> 00:27:15,756
I'll deliver Tarzan and return.

214
00:27:15,923 --> 00:27:17,551
A week's delay.

215
00:27:17,718 --> 00:27:20,220
You're in no position
to make demands, Mr. Rom.

216
00:27:20,387 --> 00:27:23,139
Your king's debt was due three months ago.

217
00:27:23,305 --> 00:27:24,390
Yes.

218
00:27:32,107 --> 00:27:35,444
I've manipulated my firm
to keep this deal alive.

219
00:27:35,610 --> 00:27:36,862
You agreed because you're going to get rich...

220
00:27:37,028 --> 00:27:38,113
so don't get emotional.

221
00:27:38,280 --> 00:27:39,531
Now you must buy us more time.

222
00:27:39,698 --> 00:27:41,824
I've bought all the time I can.

223
00:27:41,991 --> 00:27:43,159
<i>It's very simple.</i>

224
00:27:43,325 --> 00:27:44,869
You approach the arriving fleet...

225
00:27:45,035 --> 00:27:46,663
and tell them payment
must be made in Boma...

226
00:27:46,830 --> 00:27:48,498
for security reasons.

227
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:08,143
Opar had better not be a myth.

228
00:28:11,480 --> 00:28:14,566
<i>Bring me the rest,
and you'll have your mercenaries.</i>

229
00:28:14,733 --> 00:28:16,442
But if you fail this time...

230
00:28:16,609 --> 00:28:19,279
my firm will petition the Belgian parliament
for compensation.

231
00:28:19,446 --> 00:28:20,947
Your king will lose his land,

232
00:28:21,114 --> 00:28:23,116
<i>and who do you think he'll blame?</i>

233
00:28:23,283 --> 00:28:24,784
Leon Rom?

234
00:28:57,692 --> 00:28:59,069
Tarzan, you look funny.

235
00:29:00,986 --> 00:29:03,364
You said they were hooves!

236
00:29:11,748 --> 00:29:16,586
Where's my American daughter?

237
00:29:18,462 --> 00:29:19,755
<i>Wukonseeni!</i>

238
00:29:19,922 --> 00:29:21,966
<i>-Sangonini!
-Janey!</i>

239
00:29:22,717 --> 00:29:24,553
<i>-Sangonini.
-Ton moh nah nah ha lah.</i>

240
00:29:33,227 --> 00:29:34,228
<i>Keikoo!</i>

241
00:29:36,104 --> 00:29:37,605
Do you have little ones?

242
00:29:40,694 --> 00:29:42,405
You're very beautiful...

243
00:29:43,030 --> 00:29:46,408
and Wasimbu looks very funny.

244
00:29:57,586 --> 00:29:58,587
Wasimbu?

245
00:29:59,337 --> 00:30:01,088
Oh, my God!

246
00:30:04,884 --> 00:30:07,553
Janey Porter. Welcome home.

247
00:30:17,189 --> 00:30:18,731
As is our custom...

248
00:30:18,898 --> 00:30:21,275
no one entered until you returned.

249
00:30:30,243 --> 00:30:32,329
This is where Jane grew up.

250
00:30:34,122 --> 00:30:36,916
Her father taught English
in the village.

251
00:31:06,112 --> 00:31:08,906
Why do you have lookouts?

252
00:31:12,951 --> 00:31:14,869
Rumors from the south.

253
00:31:15,329 --> 00:31:17,957
Slave traders
abducting people.

254
00:31:18,124 --> 00:31:20,961
Nothing is the way it used to be.

255
00:31:21,128 --> 00:31:23,004
I'm not drinking a frothy glass...

256
00:31:23,171 --> 00:31:25,215
of something you won't tell me what it is.

257
00:31:26,299 --> 00:31:29,009
George is afraid of beer.

258
00:31:31,178 --> 00:31:32,722
<i>Everyone thinks that's funny.</i>

259
00:31:55,245 --> 00:31:57,246
They're singing the legend of Tarzan.

260
00:31:59,248 --> 00:32:02,084
For many moons
he was thought to be an evil spirit.

261
00:32:03,211 --> 00:32:04,920
<i>A ghost in the trees.</i>

262
00:32:15,389 --> 00:32:18,059
<i>They speak of his power
over the animals of the jungle.</i>

263
00:32:20,144 --> 00:32:22,353
<i>Because his spirit was forged alongside theirs.</i>

264
00:32:32,615 --> 00:32:33,783
<i>He understood them...</i>

265
00:32:36,744 --> 00:32:38,079
<i>and learned to master them.</i>

266
00:32:44,335 --> 00:32:48,089
<i>His ape mother, Kala,
loved him as her own child...</i>

267
00:32:48,254 --> 00:32:51,926
<i>and his ape brother, Akut,
treated him with kindness and respect.</i>

268
00:32:59,267 --> 00:33:01,812
He considered all men
his enemies...

269
00:33:02,437 --> 00:33:05,607
<i>because other tribes hunted
his troop as a rite of passage.</i>

270
00:33:17,410 --> 00:33:20,079
<i>Chief Muviro knew what he was like.</i>

271
00:33:20,246 --> 00:33:21,247
<i>He was kind to him...</i>

272
00:33:22,331 --> 00:33:24,666
for no man had ever started out
with less than he had.

273
00:33:51,652 --> 00:33:52,653
John?

274
00:34:04,541 --> 00:34:07,085
Blue rufous towhee.

275
00:34:08,502 --> 00:34:10,421
Mating call.

276
00:34:12,673 --> 00:34:14,759
Easy, my love.

277
00:34:26,687 --> 00:34:28,355
Mandrill.

278
00:34:29,565 --> 00:34:31,817
Also a mating call.

279
00:34:40,827 --> 00:34:42,204
Cheetah.

280
00:34:43,329 --> 00:34:44,705
Mating call.

281
00:34:45,539 --> 00:34:48,667
<i>Ten, nine...</i>

282
00:34:48,834 --> 00:34:51,711
eight, seven...

283
00:34:52,712 --> 00:34:57,093
six, five, four...

284
00:34:58,386 --> 00:34:59,553
three...

285
00:35:03,306 --> 00:35:04,349
two...

286
00:35:14,569 --> 00:35:17,239
<i>I know you took my handkerchief.</i>

287
00:35:18,697 --> 00:35:21,075
We're from America,
and my father is a teacher.

288
00:35:21,242 --> 00:35:24,078
So I wasn't raised
to believe in spirits.

289
00:35:32,753 --> 00:35:33,754
<i>Hello.</i>

290
00:35:35,922 --> 00:35:37,424
You can keep it if you like.

291
00:35:40,261 --> 00:35:42,012
Can you speak to me?

292
00:36:10,541 --> 00:36:14,211
You should know this is very unusual.

293
00:36:15,797 --> 00:36:19,967
I'm not sure my father
would find it entirely proper.

294
00:36:21,802 --> 00:36:23,554
But...

295
00:36:23,721 --> 00:36:26,974
we must make an effort
to embrace other customs...

296
00:36:29,434 --> 00:36:31,854
I don't think so, wild man!

297
00:36:34,106 --> 00:36:35,274
No. Wait!

298
00:37:56,440 --> 00:38:00,401
Let's make this easier.
Do you speak any Bantu languages?

299
00:38:01,569 --> 00:38:03,821
I speak many languages.

300
00:38:06,990 --> 00:38:08,032
Get down on your knees!

301
00:38:09,868 --> 00:38:12,747
The Kuba chief never kneels.

302
00:38:15,957 --> 00:38:18,376
<i>-Get down!
-On the ground!</i>

303
00:38:19,044 --> 00:38:20,086
<i>Aim!</i>

304
00:38:53,119 --> 00:38:54,580
<i>Hold him tight!</i>

305
00:39:00,127 --> 00:39:02,046
<i>Tighter!</i>

306
00:39:04,882 --> 00:39:07,884
You're not so strong,
you filthy ape boy.

307
00:39:22,817 --> 00:39:24,526
Some legend, Mr. Rom.

308
00:39:24,692 --> 00:39:27,238
<i>Chief Mbonga will be disappointed.</i>

309
00:39:31,742 --> 00:39:33,327
Gather ten of the strongest.

310
00:39:34,036 --> 00:39:36,330
-We'll need porters.
-Yes, sir.

311
00:39:36,706 --> 00:39:37,915
This one here, sir.

312
00:39:39,333 --> 00:39:41,335
Grab him, men.
You're coming with me!

313
00:39:53,096 --> 00:39:54,139
<i>Let go of me!</i>

314
00:39:58,603 --> 00:39:59,688
<i>John!</i>

315
00:40:06,027 --> 00:40:07,320
Let go of me!

316
00:40:07,487 --> 00:40:10,406
It must have been exciting
the first time you saw her.

317
00:40:11,907 --> 00:40:14,452
After all those black people and baboons.

318
00:40:19,372 --> 00:40:20,665
<i>John!</i>

319
00:40:49,070 --> 00:40:50,071
Quickly.

320
00:40:51,739 --> 00:40:53,907
Don't look at me. Never look at me, all right?

321
00:40:54,074 --> 00:40:56,077
Get your hands off me!

322
00:41:10,757 --> 00:41:12,760
Mr. Rom, we'll take him down.

323
00:41:12,927 --> 00:41:14,553
Go!

324
00:41:20,975 --> 00:41:22,018
Prepare the Maxim guns!

325
00:41:22,602 --> 00:41:23,603
Hurry!

326
00:41:31,111 --> 00:41:34,323
Concentrate your fire on the boilers.
Below the smokestack.

327
00:41:38,119 --> 00:41:39,495
Set sail!

328
00:41:43,082 --> 00:41:44,250
Where do you think you're going?

329
00:41:55,427 --> 00:41:57,345
I need you to scream.

330
00:41:59,806 --> 00:42:01,392
Like a damsel in distress?

331
00:42:13,236 --> 00:42:14,863
It doesn't matter.

332
00:42:15,822 --> 00:42:17,825
He's Tarzan, and you're Jane.

333
00:42:20,453 --> 00:42:21,954
He'll come for you.

334
00:42:37,511 --> 00:42:39,846
John!

335
00:42:43,809 --> 00:42:45,770
John!

336
00:43:11,045 --> 00:43:12,505
They came for me, Kwete.

337
00:43:12,671 --> 00:43:14,090
<i>This is not your doing.</i>

338
00:43:16,175 --> 00:43:17,551
Do you know who they were?

339
00:43:18,302 --> 00:43:20,971
We've heard of white men
who come to fight for money...

340
00:43:21,138 --> 00:43:22,722
and don't wear Belgian colors.

341
00:43:22,889 --> 00:43:24,225
They're called the Force Publique.

342
00:43:25,351 --> 00:43:27,853
The man I investigated in Belgium
told me about them.

343
00:43:28,020 --> 00:43:29,563
Did anyone mention a man named Rom?

344
00:43:30,815 --> 00:43:32,357
No one gave any names.

345
00:43:32,524 --> 00:43:36,611
But Leon Rom is King Leopold's
envoy to the Congo.

346
00:43:38,196 --> 00:43:39,322
Any other revelations?

347
00:43:40,365 --> 00:43:41,908
Not from me.

348
00:43:42,867 --> 00:43:44,202
Why do you say they came for you?

349
00:43:51,918 --> 00:43:55,923
The boat is going upstream,
which means it'll be slower.

350
00:43:56,091 --> 00:43:58,175
We need to reach
Mbonga's mountain before they do...

351
00:43:58,342 --> 00:44:01,262
if we take this route
through Mangani territory.

352
00:44:01,429 --> 00:44:02,513
How do you know where they're going?

353
00:44:02,680 --> 00:44:04,807
-This route...
-It's longer.

354
00:44:04,974 --> 00:44:06,725
But that's where they're building the railway.

355
00:44:06,891 --> 00:44:08,560
We can take it part of the way.

356
00:44:09,936 --> 00:44:10,937
Good.

357
00:44:12,982 --> 00:44:14,233
<i>John?</i>

358
00:44:15,109 --> 00:44:16,443
<i>John?</i>

359
00:44:17,694 --> 00:44:19,196
John!

360
00:44:20,072 --> 00:44:22,283
Fine! I'm coming with you anyway.

361
00:44:23,117 --> 00:44:24,534
You can't keep up.

362
00:44:24,701 --> 00:44:26,619
Maybe I can't keep up with Tarzan...

363
00:44:26,786 --> 00:44:28,413
but I can keep up with you.

364
00:44:28,580 --> 00:44:29,790
That would be unwise.

365
00:44:29,957 --> 00:44:31,542
<i>Falling behind means death.</i>

366
00:44:31,709 --> 00:44:33,585
I've spent half my life in the jungle.

367
00:44:33,752 --> 00:44:35,962
Besides, nobody here
is better with a rifle than I am.

368
00:44:36,129 --> 00:44:37,297
That man over there...

369
00:44:37,464 --> 00:44:39,424
<i>would be drowned by now...</i>

370
00:44:39,591 --> 00:44:41,134
if it weren't for me. And that's...

371
00:44:42,593 --> 00:44:44,012
You think I care about my life?

372
00:44:44,179 --> 00:44:45,473
Get your hands off me!

373
00:44:46,057 --> 00:44:48,392
-If you had...
-If I had what?

374
00:44:48,559 --> 00:44:50,186
Left you in England in your castle?

375
00:44:51,520 --> 00:44:55,524
They have my wife and their families.

376
00:45:47,534 --> 00:45:52,038
Whoever you are,
we're here by royal invitation.

377
00:45:52,205 --> 00:45:54,374
As guests of King Leopold.

378
00:45:55,459 --> 00:45:58,879
<i>Lady Clayton,
I arranged the invitation.</i>

379
00:46:00,172 --> 00:46:01,923
And you weren't included.

380
00:46:02,882 --> 00:46:04,884
Neither was Dr. Williams.

381
00:46:07,595 --> 00:46:12,058
A tribe of savages is trying
to kill your husband.

382
00:46:12,225 --> 00:46:14,102
Perhaps cut him up and eat him.

383
00:46:14,769 --> 00:46:16,813
<i>And I'm going to deliver him to them.</i>

384
00:46:16,980 --> 00:46:18,397
<i>And once they've killed him...</i>

385
00:46:18,565 --> 00:46:20,900
<i>Dr. Williams won't be left as a witness...</i>

386
00:46:21,067 --> 00:46:22,318
that's obvious.

387
00:46:24,697 --> 00:46:26,532
Which raises the question...

388
00:46:27,615 --> 00:46:29,493
where does that leave you?

389
00:46:36,499 --> 00:46:39,502
You'll notice none of the natives is smiling.

390
00:46:46,843 --> 00:46:48,762
Is that your friend?

391
00:46:50,930 --> 00:46:51,931
They all are.

392
00:47:00,357 --> 00:47:02,609
Did you know the right side
of your mustache...

393
00:47:02,776 --> 00:47:05,153
is lower than the left?

394
00:47:48,196 --> 00:47:49,531
<i>John!</i>

395
00:47:49,697 --> 00:47:51,282
<i>John!</i>

396
00:48:14,806 --> 00:48:15,807
Different kinds of savagery.

397
00:48:25,859 --> 00:48:28,361
How are we going to board
a train traveling at 40 miles an hour?

398
00:48:32,865 --> 00:48:34,366
Gravity.

399
00:48:50,133 --> 00:48:51,217
Damn!

400
00:49:58,369 --> 00:49:59,828
Get on my back.

401
00:50:04,999 --> 00:50:06,292
Good. Your legs, too.

402
00:50:12,047 --> 00:50:14,843
Don't think for a second
you're saving my life.

403
00:51:46,558 --> 00:51:48,769
Give me the keys to those chains.

404
00:52:03,952 --> 00:52:04,953
Are you the commanding officer?

405
00:52:05,120 --> 00:52:06,162
Don't tell him anything.

406
00:52:08,456 --> 00:52:11,376
You've got very big earlobes, son.

407
00:52:17,590 --> 00:52:19,925
You think that was a lucky shot?

408
00:52:20,884 --> 00:52:22,302
I don't speak your language.

409
00:52:22,886 --> 00:52:24,513
What's your job here?

410
00:52:26,767 --> 00:52:28,227
I work as an engineer on the bridge.

411
00:52:29,519 --> 00:52:32,355
And how many men have you enslaved
to build your bridge?

412
00:52:33,606 --> 00:52:35,150
Eight hundred.

413
00:52:35,316 --> 00:52:37,693
Plus this group of natives.

414
00:52:37,860 --> 00:52:41,030
<i>Please, I build bridges
for Monsieur Rom's new army.</i>

415
00:52:41,197 --> 00:52:42,365
I have no part in this.

416
00:52:43,534 --> 00:52:44,618
What army?

417
00:52:50,623 --> 00:52:51,666
<i>The railways...</i>

418
00:52:51,833 --> 00:52:53,835
combined with the navigable rivers...

419
00:52:54,960 --> 00:52:59,467
put 60% of the Congo
within three days of a fort.

420
00:53:01,635 --> 00:53:04,304
The forts are ready.
But the army hasn't come.

421
00:53:04,471 --> 00:53:05,722
Where are they?

422
00:53:05,889 --> 00:53:06,973
I don't know.

423
00:53:07,640 --> 00:53:09,267
They should have arrived months ago.

424
00:53:09,934 --> 00:53:12,270
But they said
the king still has to pay for them.

425
00:53:12,436 --> 00:53:13,854
How many are there?

426
00:53:16,274 --> 00:53:17,484
Twenty thousand.

427
00:53:18,985 --> 00:53:21,029
And they're no ordinary soldiers.

428
00:53:22,154 --> 00:53:23,989
They're mercenaries.

429
00:53:24,490 --> 00:53:26,576
They enjoy killing.

430
00:53:26,743 --> 00:53:28,703
Twenty thousand men, fifty forts.

431
00:53:28,870 --> 00:53:33,334
That's 400 bloodthirsty bastards,
each armed with a Maxim machine gun

432
00:53:33,501 --> 00:53:35,503
and repeating rifles.

433
00:53:35,669 --> 00:53:39,506
<i>He'll end up enslaving the entire country
to get what he wants.</i>

434
00:53:40,173 --> 00:53:41,883
The people won't have a choice.

435
00:53:43,385 --> 00:53:45,220
What does Mr. Rom have to do with this?

436
00:53:45,387 --> 00:53:48,640
It's his plan.
He's in charge of all of it.

437
00:53:51,309 --> 00:53:54,521
They expect him to be appointed
Governor-General soon.

438
00:54:02,445 --> 00:54:03,863
<i>No. Wait!</i>

439
00:54:10,036 --> 00:54:11,579
Wait! Please!

440
00:56:00,063 --> 00:56:01,231
You have to jump.

441
00:56:01,439 --> 00:56:03,024
Shut up, you idiot!

442
00:56:03,191 --> 00:56:05,485
They'll have to chase you
through the jungle at night.

443
00:56:10,864 --> 00:56:15,453
If you do anything unladylike out there...

444
00:56:16,914 --> 00:56:18,498
he drowns.

445
00:56:37,851 --> 00:56:39,477
<i>Enjoying the view?</i>

446
00:56:42,856 --> 00:56:46,234
<i>Obviously, I know
you can see me, Lady Clayton.</i>

447
00:56:59,706 --> 00:57:01,915
Are you religious, Mr. Rom?

448
00:57:02,082 --> 00:57:04,543
No. It's made from Madagascan spider silk.

449
00:57:07,881 --> 00:57:10,382
My priest bought it for me in Jerusalem...

450
00:57:10,549 --> 00:57:12,176
when I was nine years old.

451
00:57:13,052 --> 00:57:15,888
It seems you and your priest
were very close.

452
00:57:16,597 --> 00:57:17,640
Why?

453
00:57:25,399 --> 00:57:26,650
I want you to know...

454
00:57:26,817 --> 00:57:30,195
that I believe your husband
had no right to bring you here.

455
00:57:30,361 --> 00:57:31,571
I wanted to come, Mr. Rom.

456
00:57:31,738 --> 00:57:32,947
Of course.

457
00:57:33,114 --> 00:57:35,575
But he was supposed to be the only victim.

458
00:57:35,742 --> 00:57:39,537
Apart from all the people in this country
you're enslaving, obviously.

459
00:57:41,039 --> 00:57:42,040
Could you pass me the pepper?

460
00:58:15,657 --> 00:58:17,951
It also works as a necklace.

461
00:58:30,422 --> 00:58:35,468
On my last visit to Europe, I attended
a couple of lectures at the Sorbonne.

462
00:58:36,136 --> 00:58:39,096
The King of France received
a smattering of applause.

463
00:58:39,263 --> 00:58:41,432
Henry Morton Stanley,
an orphaned bastard from birth...

464
00:58:41,598 --> 00:58:43,183
received a standing ovation.

465
00:58:46,813 --> 00:58:48,689
People love a good story.

466
00:58:50,441 --> 00:58:54,027
Yes, some are born into
aristocratic families, like your husband.

467
00:58:57,656 --> 00:59:00,160
But everyone can relate
to someone born of humble beginnings...

468
00:59:00,326 --> 00:59:03,998
who saved his king from bankruptcy
and restored his nation's honor.

469
00:59:04,165 --> 00:59:06,541
That is a man who will never be forgotten.

470
00:59:11,254 --> 00:59:13,839
My king's army arrives
in six days.

471
00:59:14,256 --> 00:59:17,509
All I have left to do is
deliver Tarzan to Chief Mbonga.

472
00:59:20,221 --> 00:59:23,640
Yes, I expected you to recognize the name.

473
00:59:26,018 --> 00:59:29,189
I know he's obsessed
with killing your husband...

474
00:59:29,355 --> 00:59:31,273
but I have yet to discover why.

475
00:59:31,440 --> 00:59:33,692
What did Tarzan do?

476
00:59:35,653 --> 00:59:37,697
He killed his only son.

477
00:59:45,579 --> 00:59:50,877
And be prepared, because that's nothing
compared to what he'll do to you.

478
00:59:54,923 --> 00:59:59,886
Your husband's savagery disturbs me
more than I can express.

479
01:00:01,388 --> 01:00:03,932
Whereas the spirit
I see in you...

480
01:00:16,777 --> 01:00:19,279
Give me my knife, please.

481
01:00:20,739 --> 01:00:22,282
Slowly.

482
01:00:22,449 --> 01:00:24,451
Your friend won't float.

483
01:00:34,754 --> 01:00:36,256
Thank you.

484
01:00:46,683 --> 01:00:48,768
<i>Lady Clayton has finished.</i>

485
01:00:50,936 --> 01:00:54,898
An ordinary man can do the impossible
to save the woman he loves.

486
01:00:55,941 --> 01:00:58,737
My husband is no ordinary man.

487
01:01:28,474 --> 01:01:30,185
<i>You can't go alone.</i>

488
01:01:30,351 --> 01:01:32,562
If they see seven people,
they'll consider it an attack.

489
01:01:33,313 --> 01:01:34,980
<i>No one would survive.</i>

490
01:01:37,484 --> 01:01:40,570
It says here that it's illegal
to pay natives for their labor.

491
01:01:40,737 --> 01:01:44,239
And this is the record
of slaves taken so far.

492
01:01:44,406 --> 01:01:46,743
<i>Forced labor,
diamonds everywhere.</i>

493
01:01:46,910 --> 01:01:50,789
Rom is going to make your monarch
the richest king in the world.

494
01:01:50,955 --> 01:01:52,373
Dr. Williams, you have your evidence.

495
01:01:54,166 --> 01:01:56,251
I'll deal with Rom.

496
01:01:57,503 --> 01:01:59,963
There must be another way, John.

497
01:02:01,255 --> 01:02:03,509
I have no choice.

498
01:02:03,675 --> 01:02:05,260
I'd lose two days going around.

499
01:02:05,427 --> 01:02:07,846
-Going around what?
-The Mangani.

500
01:02:08,013 --> 01:02:10,517
Those gorillas you used to live with?

501
01:02:10,683 --> 01:02:11,851
They're not gorillas.

502
01:02:12,018 --> 01:02:14,604
Gorillas are gentle. The Mangani aren't.

503
01:02:22,695 --> 01:02:25,532
I've never abandoned a friend in trouble,
or a comrade in need.

504
01:02:25,698 --> 01:02:27,908
I'm not starting now.

505
01:02:28,075 --> 01:02:30,494
Stop calling me "Doctor".
My name's George.

506
01:02:31,120 --> 01:02:34,206
We'll keep your evidence safe, George.

507
01:02:34,373 --> 01:02:35,375
I know.

508
01:02:36,500 --> 01:02:38,085
We'll meet in Boma.

509
01:02:38,794 --> 01:02:39,795
If we don't come back...

510
01:02:39,962 --> 01:02:42,340
We will.
With their families.

511
01:02:42,507 --> 01:02:44,092
...give the books
to my grandfather in London.

512
01:02:45,718 --> 01:02:46,760
I'm not going to wait for him.

513
01:02:46,927 --> 01:02:48,178
Tell me something I don't know.

514
01:03:00,984 --> 01:03:03,402
What I'd give for a horse right now.

515
01:03:04,903 --> 01:03:06,864
Why don't people ride zebras?

516
01:03:07,489 --> 01:03:09,576
Horses kick to get away.

517
01:03:09,743 --> 01:03:11,702
Zebras keep kicking
until you're dead.

518
01:03:12,619 --> 01:03:14,163
They're pretty much the same.

519
01:03:14,329 --> 01:03:17,083
<i>It's almost impossible to tame
something born wild.</i>

520
01:03:17,250 --> 01:03:18,835
It seems to have worked with you.

521
01:03:20,419 --> 01:03:23,338
I feel silly asking...

522
01:03:24,006 --> 01:03:26,468
but can you really talk
to animals?

523
01:03:26,634 --> 01:03:30,095
You're an educated man, Mr. Williams.
You tell me.

524
01:03:41,440 --> 01:03:43,150
Hello, beautiful!

525
01:03:46,029 --> 01:03:48,031
That's a male.

526
01:03:48,198 --> 01:03:49,949
He's warning us
not to get too close.

527
01:03:51,117 --> 01:03:54,788
Are you trying to tell me
those things are deadly?

528
01:03:54,954 --> 01:03:56,705
If you get too close,
he'll knock you down...

529
01:03:56,872 --> 01:03:59,167
and gut you with those three-inch claws.

530
01:04:00,752 --> 01:04:02,337
No ostrich is going to kill me.

531
01:04:03,421 --> 01:04:05,047
Try riding one like a horse.

532
01:04:06,090 --> 01:04:07,883
You'd like to see that, wouldn't you?

533
01:04:24,817 --> 01:04:28,529
<i>Damn! That smells terrible. God!</i>

534
01:04:28,695 --> 01:04:30,489
<i>Keep your voice down.</i>

535
01:04:34,869 --> 01:04:36,287
Is that them?

536
01:04:42,626 --> 01:04:44,086
You don't want to do that, George.

537
01:04:44,670 --> 01:04:47,507
If you kill one,
the others won't stop until they tear you apart.

538
01:04:49,925 --> 01:04:51,468
So what do you want to do?

539
01:04:52,053 --> 01:04:55,348
Their leader will come,
and we'll fight.

540
01:04:57,182 --> 01:04:58,892
You're going to fight one of them?

541
01:04:59,059 --> 01:05:02,187
<i>I'd rather not. I have to face Akut.</i>

542
01:05:03,898 --> 01:05:05,816
He was once my brother.

543
01:05:06,483 --> 01:05:07,985
Now he'll see me as a deserter.

544
01:05:08,610 --> 01:05:11,989
Whatever happens, don't interfere.

545
01:05:12,865 --> 01:05:14,116
Understood?

546
01:05:14,283 --> 01:05:15,283
Yes.

547
01:05:20,999 --> 01:05:22,208
Good Lord.

548
01:05:25,295 --> 01:05:26,713
Is that Akut?

549
01:05:27,714 --> 01:05:29,924
Are you sure you don't want me
to shoot him?

550
01:05:33,719 --> 01:05:35,179
Promise me.

551
01:06:59,639 --> 01:07:01,598
Get down on your knees and bow.

552
01:07:02,142 --> 01:07:03,977
<i>Now!</i>

553
01:07:04,644 --> 01:07:06,646
Expose your most vulnerable parts!

554
01:07:22,369 --> 01:07:23,996
Want me to lick his balls, too?

555
01:07:24,163 --> 01:07:25,664
If you want to live.

556
01:07:27,290 --> 01:07:28,499
<i>George...</i>

557
01:07:28,666 --> 01:07:29,834
I wasn't going to do it!

558
01:07:30,835 --> 01:07:32,171
It looked like you were.

559
01:07:51,858 --> 01:07:53,192
<i>Is he coming back?</i>

560
01:07:53,442 --> 01:07:56,820
No. That seemed pretty conclusive.

561
01:07:56,987 --> 01:07:59,448
If that bite gets infected,
you'll be in trouble.

562
01:08:00,032 --> 01:08:01,033
Then there's this.

563
01:08:05,746 --> 01:08:08,124
You know I wasn't going to lick his...

564
01:08:08,291 --> 01:08:10,293
-It looked like you were.
-No, it didn't.

565
01:08:14,963 --> 01:08:18,550
You're not going anywhere
until we stitch this up.

566
01:08:24,431 --> 01:08:26,225
Are you eating those?

567
01:08:26,725 --> 01:08:27,976
They taste like bacon.

568
01:08:29,185 --> 01:08:31,397
If you find a nice fat snake,
I'll eat it.

569
01:08:31,563 --> 01:08:34,858
Snake meat is good.
I'm not eating a damn ant.

570
01:08:35,025 --> 01:08:36,777
Suit yourself.

571
01:08:37,945 --> 01:08:39,322
And when you're done...

572
01:08:39,489 --> 01:08:42,784
chew the flowers into a poultice
and apply it to the wound with rubber vine.

573
01:08:44,077 --> 01:08:45,537
It'll stay in place.

574
01:08:48,539 --> 01:08:49,581
Do you realize you always say...

575
01:08:49,748 --> 01:08:51,709
you won't do something right before doing it?

576
01:08:51,875 --> 01:08:53,837
Do I?

577
01:08:54,003 --> 01:08:55,879
Only since I joined you.

578
01:08:59,466 --> 01:09:00,759
How's it coming along?

579
01:09:00,926 --> 01:09:02,553
I can manage.

580
01:09:03,178 --> 01:09:04,470
Pain...

581
01:09:06,681 --> 01:09:08,808
I have far too much experience with it.

582
01:09:16,567 --> 01:09:18,945
The Civil War was a hard fight.

583
01:09:20,572 --> 01:09:22,448
But I was proud to be part of it.

584
01:09:24,825 --> 01:09:26,619
Although it changed me.

585
01:09:27,662 --> 01:09:31,331
Afterward,
I was a walking time bomb.

586
01:09:34,126 --> 01:09:37,296
I joined up to fight
against Maximilian in Mexico.

587
01:09:39,172 --> 01:09:42,759
Then I made a living fighting
the Native Americans.

588
01:09:44,095 --> 01:09:46,264
I did both of those things for money.

589
01:09:48,640 --> 01:09:50,392
<i>I was young.</i>

590
01:09:51,226 --> 01:09:53,103
But that's no excuse.

591
01:09:58,274 --> 01:09:59,860
Mexico was tough.

592
01:10:01,987 --> 01:10:04,406
But what we did to the Native Americans...

593
01:10:14,165 --> 01:10:16,168
I'm no better than those Belgians.

594
01:10:45,696 --> 01:10:47,198
My old friend.

595
01:11:03,716 --> 01:11:07,511
<i>The Teke say an elephant's eyes
speak the finest language.</i>

596
01:11:10,305 --> 01:11:13,100
Who else can make you feel so much
without saying a word?

597
01:12:33,473 --> 01:12:36,600
Is Mr. Rom ready
to continue his seductive conversation?

598
01:12:37,810 --> 01:12:41,563
He was the only reason
you weren't subdued.

599
01:12:41,730 --> 01:12:43,357
Subdued?

600
01:12:43,523 --> 01:12:45,484
Is that what your mother would call it?

601
01:12:49,363 --> 01:12:52,116
Promise me you won't stay and fight.

602
01:12:52,281 --> 01:12:53,324
Shut your mouth!

603
01:12:53,491 --> 01:12:56,369
Can you rally the tribes?

604
01:13:07,172 --> 01:13:10,341
<i>Stop the boat!</i>

605
01:13:10,591 --> 01:13:11,968
Turn around!

606
01:13:33,783 --> 01:13:36,702
Hold your fire! You'll hit the girl!

607
01:13:48,880 --> 01:13:50,257
What a woman.

608
01:14:21,412 --> 01:14:22,413
Wait!

609
01:14:23,457 --> 01:14:26,293
They'll follow my tracks.
Go get help.

610
01:14:26,585 --> 01:14:27,586
Go!

611
01:15:59,177 --> 01:16:01,137
Will you promise not to hurt them?

612
01:16:18,113 --> 01:16:19,531
No! Stop!

613
01:16:37,674 --> 01:16:39,468
<i>Please, stop!</i>

614
01:16:41,053 --> 01:16:41,886
John!

615
01:16:50,395 --> 01:16:53,065
<i>Stop!</i>

616
01:16:59,779 --> 01:17:00,988
Stop! Please!

617
01:17:04,534 --> 01:17:06,703
Please!

618
01:17:06,869 --> 01:17:07,912
Come on!

619
01:17:08,079 --> 01:17:09,372
<i>Fall back!</i>

620
01:19:01,151 --> 01:19:03,237
<i>What was that?</i>

621
01:19:03,862 --> 01:19:04,905
<i>Tarzan.</i>

622
01:19:05,614 --> 01:19:07,783
Though he sounded different
from what I expected.

623
01:19:09,118 --> 01:19:10,119
<i>Better.</i>

624
01:19:24,716 --> 01:19:25,842
Schmidt-Rubin.

625
01:19:31,931 --> 01:19:33,141
John!

626
01:19:35,226 --> 01:19:36,229
<i>John!</i>

627
01:19:37,063 --> 01:19:38,063
<i>Stop!</i>

628
01:22:01,914 --> 01:22:03,166
<i>John!</i>

629
01:22:10,673 --> 01:22:13,635
You feel my pain.

630
01:23:21,452 --> 01:23:22,453
<i>I am not your enemy.</i>

631
01:23:22,620 --> 01:23:24,580
Rom will buy an army with your jewels.

632
01:23:24,747 --> 01:23:27,291
-They can't touch us!
-You know nothing!

633
01:23:27,458 --> 01:23:30,461
Your lands are full of diamonds.
He'll come back for them.

634
01:24:01,160 --> 01:24:02,910
Your chief is under my control!

635
01:24:03,661 --> 01:24:05,121
<i>You're all under my control!</i>

636
01:24:07,206 --> 01:24:08,833
Finish him.

637
01:24:09,000 --> 01:24:10,375
Finish him!

638
01:24:10,542 --> 01:24:11,668
Stop!

639
01:24:18,801 --> 01:24:21,722
Your son killed the only one
who ever cared for me.

640
01:24:21,889 --> 01:24:22,931
She was an animal.

641
01:24:23,098 --> 01:24:25,851
She was my mother.

642
01:24:29,062 --> 01:24:30,605
<i>How could he have known?</i>

643
01:24:32,524 --> 01:24:35,110
My son was just a boy!

644
01:24:35,778 --> 01:24:37,528
Not like you!

645
01:24:40,573 --> 01:24:42,700
Where was your honor?

646
01:24:54,921 --> 01:24:56,589
I had no honor.

647
01:24:58,841 --> 01:25:00,176
I had no honor.

648
01:25:39,841 --> 01:25:42,385
<i>Can we please stop this?</i>

649
01:25:42,552 --> 01:25:46,264
We won't kill all of you,
and you won't kill all of us.

650
01:25:46,431 --> 01:25:47,514
But Rom will.

651
01:25:48,432 --> 01:25:50,434
<i>He'll wipe you out.</i>

652
01:25:50,600 --> 01:25:54,021
<i>Every last one of you.
As if you never existed.</i>

653
01:25:56,733 --> 01:25:58,861
You think that's impossible?

654
01:25:59,486 --> 01:26:02,030
You're looking at a man
who's seen it with his own eyes.

655
01:27:12,558 --> 01:27:15,310
PORT OF BOMA

656
01:27:35,833 --> 01:27:37,084
Rom has the diamonds.

657
01:27:37,251 --> 01:27:40,045
There are a thousand men there.
Twenty thousand more on the way.

658
01:27:40,921 --> 01:27:42,004
We're finished.

659
01:27:42,171 --> 01:27:43,214
Where are you going?

660
01:27:43,381 --> 01:27:44,716
To find some friends.

661
01:28:03,817 --> 01:28:05,445
Everyone to your posts!

662
01:29:02,584 --> 01:29:05,462
It doesn't matter. It's too late.

663
01:29:16,517 --> 01:29:18,060
Move!

664
01:29:18,227 --> 01:29:19,435
<i>Fall back!</i>

665
01:29:45,712 --> 01:29:47,296
Bring her!

666
01:30:15,741 --> 01:30:16,783
Take the diamonds.

667
01:30:18,537 --> 01:30:19,663
Run!

668
01:30:21,956 --> 01:30:24,917
John! No! John!

669
01:30:27,253 --> 01:30:28,254
<i>John!</i>

670
01:30:53,405 --> 01:30:55,490
Get to the ship! Move!

671
01:31:07,585 --> 01:31:09,212
We're coming! Hold on!

672
01:31:56,385 --> 01:31:57,552
John?

673
01:32:09,020 --> 01:32:10,439
<i>That's the payment.</i>

674
01:32:12,984 --> 01:32:15,152
Lower the skiff and don't waste time.

675
01:32:21,784 --> 01:32:24,453
<i>Did you see him? Is he down there?</i>

676
01:32:31,170 --> 01:32:34,672
Maxim machine gun, model 1886.

677
01:32:37,258 --> 01:32:39,969
This is much better than my Walker Colts.

678
01:34:01,466 --> 01:34:02,509
<i>Mr. Rom!</i>

679
01:34:05,430 --> 01:34:07,098
Mr. Frum, you fat coward!

680
01:34:07,265 --> 01:34:09,350
<i>Come and get your diamonds!</i>

681
01:34:16,357 --> 01:34:18,525
<i>Farewell, sweet lord.</i>

682
01:34:20,819 --> 01:34:22,863
Take Tarzan with you.

683
01:34:24,657 --> 01:34:26,534
The future belongs to me.

684
01:34:49,515 --> 01:34:50,641
What did you say?

685
01:34:53,393 --> 01:34:54,644
Mating call.

686
01:35:42,442 --> 01:35:43,653
Tarzan!

687
01:35:44,320 --> 01:35:45,947
Tarzan!

688
01:35:47,865 --> 01:35:48,741
John!

689
01:35:49,826 --> 01:35:50,660
John!

690
01:37:09,781 --> 01:37:11,949
We're not getting involved
in that mess for nothing.

691
01:37:14,910 --> 01:37:16,244
Raise the anchor!

692
01:38:00,331 --> 01:38:02,250
It's incredible.

693
01:38:13,009 --> 01:38:14,512
Nice outfit, Janey.

694
01:38:17,889 --> 01:38:19,891
It looked like you were
about to give me a hug.

695
01:38:21,351 --> 01:38:22,936
I wasn't going to.

696
01:38:24,187 --> 01:38:25,939
It looked like it.

697
01:38:52,007 --> 01:38:54,301
<i>An open letter
to His Serene Majesty...</i>

698
01:38:54,468 --> 01:38:58,931
<i>Leopold II, King of the Belgians.</i>

699
01:38:59,098 --> 01:39:00,682
<i>Every charge I'm about to make...</i>

700
01:39:00,849 --> 01:39:03,226
<i>against Your Majesty's
personal government of the Congo...</i>

701
01:39:03,393 --> 01:39:05,813
<i>has been subjected
to thorough investigation.</i>

702
01:39:05,980 --> 01:39:10,859
<i>We have witnesses, documents,
letters and official records...</i>

703
01:39:11,026 --> 01:39:14,654
<i>to present to you
and to substantiate our accusations...</i>

704
01:39:14,821 --> 01:39:20,243
<i>of your systematic abuse
and enslavement of the Congolese people.</i>

705
01:39:22,746 --> 01:39:27,584
<i>George Washington Williams.
July 18, 1890.</i>

706
01:39:55,612 --> 01:39:58,281
ONE YEAR LATER

707
01:40:39,072 --> 01:40:41,783
<i>They're singing the legend of Tarzan.</i>

708
01:40:49,207 --> 01:40:52,502
<i>For many moons
he was thought to be an evil spirit.</i>

709
01:40:54,129 --> 01:40:55,921
<i>A ghost in the trees.</i>

710
01:41:02,346 --> 01:41:06,183
<i>They speak of his power
over the animals of the jungle.</i>

711
01:41:07,894 --> 01:41:10,354
<i>Because his spirit was forged alongside theirs.</i>

712
01:41:13,523 --> 01:41:15,150
<i>He understood them...</i>

713
01:41:23,533 --> 01:41:26,036
<i>and learned to become one with them.</i>

714
01:44:30,106 --> 01:44:34,803
FOR JERRY
